Z||000004||theogre||10-01-1999||08:58 PM||||which AL wheel are you after? there are allot of them in the salvage yards around here. Some are in pretty good shape.<P>There are a bunch of GM/Pontiac styles around that fit. For what you are saying it costs to rework them, I can find probably 2  whole sets of them in desent shape. I'm likely going after one set tomorow. Saw them last week, but didn't have time.... Those 5 spoke "tech" style wheels are all over the place. If you look around some of the newer styles are also to be had for decent prices. Especially if you can find an insurance wrecker that deals parts. These yards handle allot more new cars than normal yards.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||10-01-1999||09:37 PM||||burdell1,<P>That may have been me. I had my wheels done but it was done at a friends machine shop and I paid him $40.00 a wheel, his shop is not a auto type machine shop. He does antique metal fabrication and restoration. And from what I understand he chucked the wheels up on a lathe and turned  and polished them. He did this in less than a day. Pic of the car is on DaRkLoRD's web page   ( not that good of a pic of the wheels but they look real nice.<P>Fieroshop has also seen this car. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fastonpumpgas||10-01-1999||10:28 PM||||My wheels look real bad too, I plan to polish them myself, I beleive I can do it without derimming them, although prolly have to get the rebalance, I want the weights in the back anyway. <A HREF="http://www.sentex.net/~bmcf/Fiero/Polish.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sentex.net/~bmcf/Fiero/Polish.htm</A>    This will give you an idea what you are in for, if all else fail I think you can Firebirds wheels will fit ????<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||10-02-1999||02:01 AM||||Just went through this myself,and wound up getting a set of 16X7Enkie racing wheels(lightweight)that actually don't look bad,for $80 apiece on a closeout.<BR>Started out looking for some factory take-offs,but the insurance companies(consumers)have driven the prices up beyond what a full set is worth,and with three different bolt patterns,the only ones that'll fit are from the Grand Am(16X6),or Chev Baretta(15X7).<P>If you're not really motivated,you could turn your wheels on the drive wheel of your car(!)raised on blocks,and next to something(tree,wall)not easily moved,for safety.<BR>Doing just the lip,and rim,then painting the center portion should go pretty fast.<BR>I polished a set of wheels by hand-held buffers,sandpaper once,took 8 hours per wheel,from rough castings;a real pain.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ggarsk||10-03-1999||04:23 AM||||I'm in the process of polishing my wheels. I got 2 extra rims so that I can take my time. One is done and it looks great. But it is a lot of work. It helps that I have a air compresser and die grinder. That makes it easier then doing it all by hand. ||||reg||1
