Z||000010||DISenchanted||10-03-1999||03:07 PM||||I happen to polish wheels in my free time (which is getting smaller by the day).  The easiest and fastest way to polish the rims would be to get a drill (air or electric), a die grinder will work as well, as lond as you can vary the speed of it.  Then get a few 2in Scotch bright pads (the round type with the threading on the back) both 3m and standard abrasives have them.  The reason that the factory rims are so hard to polish is that they are either anadized or clear coated, there are only two ways to remove this coating chemically or just cutting through it.<P>If you choose to do i chemically, it will take about half the time and cost twice as much.  You buy some anadizer remover and apply it as directed, the when the coating is gone use the round pads on the drill.  They come in different grits so use the coarser ones first.  When you have finished with the pads, the wheel should have a slight satin finish.  Then take a metal polish(blue magic works the best) and apply it with a soft buff.  For the aluminum rims that came on my 85 se it took me about 4 hours total and cost about 40 dollars.  If you need a place to get all this stuff go to <A HREF="http://www.pfpi.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pfpi.com</A>  he ships everywhere and the prices are very reasonable.  Just tell him what you plan on doing and he'll take you step by step.<P>One final thing, if anyone lives in/near California and wants their rims done just email me.<P>Good luck.||||reg||3
Z||000011||88 Fiero||10-03-1999||03:25 PM||||check batboy's website...My '88 Fiero have 17 inch all around.The only thing tho is that on '88 you have to shave at least 5 millimeters to make it fit in the front...the rear is okay...Oh yeah...this are OZ Competition wheels...Also, I don't have these anymore..sold it to a friend..I ordered new ones which is also 17 inch five spokes..almost Ferrari style but the rear is 17 X 8 1/2 and the fronts are 17 X 7 1/2... You can shove 18 on a Fiero but it will drag your bearings. I still need to shave the front again and I think I might need to go coils over in the rear...but I have to check to see if it's going to fit with the stock struts....I think if you are planning to all out fixing up your car...just save the extra money and buy the best looking wheel you could find...that's how I did..it took awhile to save it but it's worth...I get alot of stares on my car...{=o)<p>[This message has been edited by 88 Fiero (edited 10-03-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-03-1999||06:10 PM||||I chem stripped the wheels on my pinto several years ago..... I never recoated them at all..... just hit them with some Mothers a couple times a year.<P>I'm thinking of doing the same thing to my fiero wheels. Except with them I may completly stip them, and powder coat the gray back in. Then Mothers the rest.<P>Interesting thing with the ford wheels was that the chem strip didn't pull off the color..... Maybe they where powder coated.... I always thought chem strip would pull of powder too. Guess it depends on what stripper you use.<P>BTW.... don't try to use Mothers or similar product with a buffing wheel. It doesn't work well... the stuff really works best with simple hand buffing.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DISenchanted||10-04-1999||01:50 PM||||Orge,<P>that's why I said to use Blue Magic.  Mothers is still too abrasive to use with a buffing wheel.  Try Blue Magic.  If you want, I'll send you some.  I get it free.||||reg||1
